{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1556719662,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU0MzkxNzY1MDg=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3371","origin":"github"},"title":"Ability to \"tag\"/annotate commits","message":"Per our discussion and as mentioned in #3370, in particular in the case of run/rerun and repository description rendering (from `run` records) #3358, we would benefit from mechanism to annotate the commits with loosely defined tags.  The immediate usecases I see is having tags such as `curation`, `preprocessing`, `analysis` but then could be more or less specific and multiple per commit.\n\nSome discussion points\n- those tags should not be a part of the commit message so they could be changed later on e.g. to harmonize etc\n\nPossible implementations:\n1.  a file sidecar under datalad control (e.g. `.datalad/history_metadata.json`) where one of the sections could be `tags` and contain a dictionary of tags pointing to list of commits\n   - `datalad run` then could get an option `--tags`  or alike to add a record (a separate commit) into that file pointing to the \"run\" commits\n   - `datalad rerun` could then consume a list of tags to \"rerun\"\n2. [git notes](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-notes)?\n\nproblems with such sidecar-file (`1.` above):\n- since it is not built-in git feature, rewriting git history might invalidate stored in the list commits.  But I do not think it would be a frequent use-case.  If someone consciously rewriting immediate recent history, that already might invalidate the entirety of `run` recorded provenance anyways.","files":null}]}